        "b_sci":  "Mylopharyngodon piceus",
        "b_first":  "Imported 1970s–80s for aquaculture snail control; escaped into Mississippi basin. By 2003, a commercial fisher captured the first official wild black carp in the U.S. in Horseshoe Lake, Illinois—an oxbow lake that connects to the Mississippi River during high waters.",
        "b_path":  "Black carp first entered the U.S. unintentionally, mixed into imported stocks of grass carp arriving from Asia. Because juveniles of both species look nearly identical, the introduction went largely unnoticed. Commercial fish farmers in the South later utilized black carp as a biological control agent against parasitic worms that use snails for part of its lifecycle, because black carp are molluscivorous (eat mollusks like snails). Flooding ~1994 caused aquaculture ponds to overflow into adjacent river basins allowing black carp to escape directly into the Mississippi Basin. While federal law bans their transport, they are naturally expanding deeper into the U.S. river systems.",
        "b_impacts":  "Crushes \u0026 eats native mussels and snails—many already imperiled; threatens freshwater mollusk diversity.",
        "b_detect":  "Commercial-harvest reports, netting, molecular detection (environmental DNA / eDNA); heavy pharyngeal teeth are diagnostic.",
        "b_manage":  "Monitoring \u0026 removal, require sterile (triploid) stock, barriers; USFWS injurious listing.",
